diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — "switch near the other rack keeps rebooting, connection keeps flapping" — cannot be confirmed by any direct evidence in the live lab.

**Affected component:** No subsystem is implicated. All layers were inspected: L2 identity (infra_sweep, l2_snapshot), L3 addressing and ARP (infra_sweep), BGP control plane (bgp_snapshot), process/resource pressure (pressure_sweep), and temporal link/DHCP history (dhcp_link_history).

**Mechanism:** No flap mechanism exists. A link-flap fault requires either an active flap script/process or carrier-change counters that exceed the startup baseline. Every interface on every router — including `leaf_router_0_1`, the leaf device adjacent to `pc_0_1` ("the other machine") — shows exactly `carrier_changes=2`, which is the standard Kathara container startup sequence (interface initializes down, then comes up when the virtual cable is attached). No additional transitions have occurred since startup.

**Localization:** No device is faulty. BGP sessions across all five routers (`leaf_router_0_0`, `leaf_router_0_1`, `spine_router_0_0`, `spine_router_0_1`, `super_spine_router_0`) are fully Established. End-to-end reachability between `pc_0_0` and `pc_0_1` is 0% loss in both directions.

**Root cause:** There is no fault present; the reported intermittent flapping leaves no mechanism, no historical trace, and no current impairment in this lab instance.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required. If the user experiences the symptom again, capturing the timestamp of the next flap event and re-running `get_reachability` immediately afterward would provide the evidence needed to confirm and localize the fault.
=== END ===